Martensite is widely recognized for its displacive transformation feature and coherence with PTMC in crystallography. Therefore, we begin with martensite single variant to establish a robust reference for subsequent bainite studies.  Fig. 2 
                         (a) is the inverse pole figure (IPF) map of the transformation product in the area where the in-situ DHM measurement was conducted. Judging from the apparent morphology, the microstructure is identified as lath martensite. The reconstructed prior austenite grains are shown in  Fig. 2 (b). The prior austenite grain on the right-hand side has an annealing twin whose grain boundary is also indicated by the black line in  Fig. 2 (a). Thus, only the upper part of the prior austenite grain was analyzed in the present study.  Fig. 2 (c)–(e) shows the martensite grew within the prior austenite grain investigated in detail. In  Fig. 2 (c), V1, V2, V4, and V8 are highlighted in yellow, blue, green, and red, respectively, based on the  

